@using TakOne.WebUI.Components
@using System.Globalization

@* <html> lang/dir are DYNAMIC: Persian (fa-IR) → RTL, English (en-US) → LTR.
   The previous version hardcoded `lang="fa" dir="rtl"` so English mode
   still rendered right-to-left, which is wrong. Reading from
   CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture means the attributes are correct on the
   very first server render (the localization middleware has already
   set CurrentUICulture based on the cookie/query-string/default before
   App.razor renders).

   - TwoLetterISOLanguageName → "fa" for fa-IR, "en" for en-US
   - TextInfo.IsRightToLeft  → true for fa-IR (Persian is RTL),
                                false for en-US (English is LTR) *@
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="@HtmlLang" dir="@HtmlDir">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <base href="/" />

    <RadzenTheme Theme="standard-dark" />

    <link rel="stylesheet" href="_content/Radzen.Blazor/Radzen.Blazor.css" />
    @* SELF-HOSTED Vazirmatn (was: Google Fonts @import inside the Tak theme
       files). Loaded BEFORE app.css + theme overrides so the font-family
       tokens everywhere resolve to the local files. Serves the app's own
       origin: works on air-gapped intranets, no third-party CDN requests,
       no DNS/TLS handshake tax on first paint. See /css/fonts.css. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/fonts.css?v=1" />
    @* BUGFIX (Issue #08 — UI): href was "app.css" (missing "css/" prefix).
       The actual file lives at wwwroot/css/app.css. Without the prefix,
       the browser requests /app.css → 404 → global styles (KPI card
       shadows, layout polish) silently fail to load. The 404 is
       indistinguishable from "file doesn't exist" in DevTools, which is
       why this went unnoticed — the page renders, just without the
       global overrides. CSS files in wwwroot subdirectories MUST
       include the subdirectory in the href. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/app.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/sidebar.css?v=2" />
    @* ?v=13 — bump cache-busting query string after adding the
       .tm-active-filters / .tm-filter-chip rules (search & category
       clear chips on the Products page). Without this bump, browsers
       keep serving the previously-cached Products.css?v=12 (which predates
       the chip rules), so the chips render as plain unstyled rectangles
       with vertically-stacked icon/text/X instead of a horizontal pill. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/Pages/Products.css?v=13" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/Pages/Dashboard.css?v=4" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/Pages/CartBudgetBar.css?v=1" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="TakOne.WebUI.styles.css" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="TakLogo.png" />

    @* FOUC-prevention script — extracted to wwwroot/js/theme.js.
       Runs synchronously in <head>. With <RadzenTheme> above, the theme
       <link> is injected by Blazor's HeadOutlet AFTER hydration — so this
       script uses a MutationObserver to detect when the link appears, then
       swaps its href to the saved theme (and layers the tak-light or
       tak-dark override on top if needed). The ?v=12 cache-bust forces the
       browser to fetch the latest theme.js (now includes tak-dark registry). *@
    <script src="js/theme.js?v=15"></script>

    @* SignalR client — VENDORED locally (Issue #07).
       Previously loaded from cdnjs with no integrity attribute. Two problems:
         (a) SECURITY — if cdnjs were ever compromised, an attacker could
             inject arbitrary JS into every TakOne user's browser, running
             with full page-origin privileges (reading non-HttpOnly cookies,
             making authenticated API calls, exfiltrating data, keylogging).
             CDNs have been compromised before — cdnjs itself had a near-miss
             in 2018. The SRI hash below pins the file: any tampering causes
             the browser to refuse to execute it (the <script> fails to load,
             which is the safer failure mode — the notificationHub.js bridge
             catches the missing `signalR` global and logs a warning instead
             of executing attacker code).
         (b) AVAILABILITY — cdnjs is intermittently blocked in Iran, the
             primary audience (fa-IR is the default culture). With the CDN
             URL, real users saw a broken notification hub whenever the
             CDN was unreachable. Vendoring the file serves it from our own
             origin, so it's always available as long as TakOne itself is.

       WHY @microsoft/signalr@8.0.0 (npm) instead of cdnjs:
         cdnjs's signalr.min.js IS the npm package's UMD bundle (cdnjs
         mirrors npm). Downloading from unpkg.com (the npm CDN) gets the
         exact same bytes that `npm install @microsoft/signalr@8.0.0`
         would put in node_modules. The SHA-256 of this file is
         fa4ed1a6561e0596b5c31df36f47c16926115fac45dcf02fab50a0a79e580b4e
         if you want to cross-verify against the npm registry.

       WHY crossorigin="anonymous" on a SAME-ORIGIN script:
         Per the SRI spec, integrity checks require the request to be in
         CORS mode. For same-origin resources, `crossorigin="anonymous"`
         opts the request into CORS mode WITHOUT triggering an actual
         preflight (same-origin requests don't need one). Without this
         attribute, some browsers silently skip the integrity check on
         same-origin scripts — defeating the whole point of SRI. Always
         pair `integrity=` with `crossorigin="anonymous"`.

       WHY ?v=8.0.0:
         Cache-bust query string. If we ever upgrade the SignalR client,
         bumping the version (e.g. to ?v=8.0.7) forces browsers to fetch
         the new file AND reminds the developer to recompute the SRI hash
         (a mismatch is the security guarantee — a stale hash on a new
         file fails closed, refusing to load).

       HOW TO UPDATE THE SIGNALR CLIENT:
         1. Download the new version:
              curl -fsSL -o wwwroot/lib/microsoft-signalr/signalr.min.js \
                https://unpkg.com/@microsoft/signalr@<NEW_VERSION>/dist/browser/signalr.min.js
              curl -fsSL -o wwwroot/lib/microsoft-signalr/signalr.min.js.map \
                https://unpkg.com/@microsoft/signalr@<NEW_VERSION>/dist/browser/signalr.min.js.map
         2. Recompute the SRI hash:
              openssl dgst -sha384 -binary wwwroot/lib/microsoft-signalr/signalr.min.js | openssl base64 -A
         3. Update the integrity attribute below with `sha384-<hash>`.
         4. Bump ?v= below to the new version.
         The .map file is for dev-tools source mapping only — it's not
         security-relevant. *@
    <script src="lib/microsoft-signalr/signalr.min.js?v=8.0.0"
            integrity="sha384-/taWmisziXYpcfnYsumSUmNaiMvG/fF/OJOUCLnqCIYTrpOZy7WbFF6FfIxwOrfL"
            crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
    @* Hub bridge — depends on the `signalR` global exposed by the script
       above. Loaded after signalr.min.js so the global is available when
       notificationHub.js's IIFE runs. *@
    <script src="js/notificationHub.js?v=10"></script>

    @* Shop page (Products) — Ctrl+F focuses the search bar (replaces the
        HTML's Ctrl+K binding). Registered globally; the listener only
        intercepts when the shop search input is present, so other pages
        keep the browser's native find-in-page behavior.

        v5 cache-bust (Round 18-D / Brutal Code Review v3 finding #24,
        part 1): added the `clearSearch` function so Products.razor's
        ClearFilters / ClearSearchAsync handlers can call
        `JS.InvokeVoidAsync("tmShopPage.clearSearch")` instead of the
        previous `JS.InvokeVoidAsync("eval", "document.getElementById(...) &&
        (document.getElementById(...).value = '')")`. Without this
        cache-bust browsers keep serving the old v4 products.js which has
        no `clearSearch` member on `window.tmShopPage`, so the new .razor
        call site would throw `JSCallException: 'tmShopPage.clearSearch'
        not found`. *@
    <script src="js/products.js?v=5"></script>

    @* Culture cookie helper — Round 18-D / Brutal Code Review v3 finding
        #24, part 1. Replaces 3 `JS.InvokeVoidAsync("eval",
        "document.cookie='takone_culture=...;path=/;max-age=31536000;
        samesite=strict'")` call sites (MobileLayout.razor:362,
        MainLayout.razor:676, Products.razor:1655) with a named JS
        function `takone.setCultureCookie(culture)` that builds the
        cookie value internally (defense-in-depth — the .razor call site
        passes ONLY a short culture string like "fa-IR"/"en-US", never a
        full cookie string that could be malformed/attacker-controlled).
        See the file's header comment for the full rationale. *@
    <script src="js/culture.js?v=1"></script>

